OIL config files
================

OIL needs a config file because it compiles generated C++ code outside the
application build system. Debug information tells OIL what a type looks like in
the target binary, but it does not tell OIL how to compile headers that the
generated code includes. The config file supplies the missing compile context:
container descriptions, pass-through type declarations, and compiler header
search paths.

The config file is TOML. Paths may be absolute, or relative to the directory
containing the config file.


Generating a config
-------------------

For local development, generate a config from the compiler you intend OIL to
use:

```
./tools/config_gen.py -c clang++ build/testing.oid.toml
```

`config_gen.py` asks the compiler for its include search paths and writes them
to the `[headers]` section. Unless `--skip-types` is used, it also starts from
`dev.oid.toml`, which lists OI's built-in container descriptions.

The generated file is a starting point. When introspecting a binary from
another source tree, add that project's include roots to `user_paths`. For
example, generated code for RocksDB container descriptors may include headers
such as `util/autovector.h` or `util/core_local.h`, so a local RocksDB checkout
needs:

```
[headers]
user_paths = [
  "/home/jon/Code/rocksdb",
]
```

Some project headers also require build-specific preprocessor defines. Those
defines cannot be recovered reliably from DWARF, so add them explicitly to
`preprocessor_defines` using local knowledge of how that binary was built:

```
[headers]
user_paths = [
  "/home/jon/Code/rocksdb",
]
preprocessor_defines = [
  "ROCKSDB_PLATFORM_POSIX=1",
  "OS_LINUX=1",
]
```

OIL emits these defines at the top of the generated C++ file, before any
headers are included.


SDK config
----------

For installed or relocatable SDK use, `config_gen.py` can copy the container
descriptions and discovered include roots under an SDK prefix and emit paths
relative to the generated config:

```
./tools/config_gen.py -c clang++ \
  --sdk-root /tmp/oil-sdk \
  sdk.oid.toml
```

This creates a bundle suitable for passing to OIL after the SDK is moved as a
unit.


Fields
------

`[types] containers`
  A list of TOML files describing containers OIL should recognize specially.
  Each container descriptor names a C++ type, its container category, required
  header, template shape, and code generation snippets.

  Example:

  ```
  [types]
  containers = [
    "types/vector_type.toml",
    "types/rocksdb_autovector_type.toml",
  ]
  ```

  Without a matching container descriptor, OIL falls back to the physical class
  layout from debug information. That is often correct for ordinary structs,
  but it is usually wrong for logical containers because their dynamic contents
  are hidden behind implementation details.

`[types] pass_through`
  A list of two-element arrays: `[type_name, header]`.

  Pass-through types are not described as containers. Instead, OIL emits the
  requested header and lets the generated C++ refer to the existing type name.
  This is useful for helper types that must be syntactically available to
  compile generated code but do not need bespoke traversal logic.

  Example:

  ```
  [types]
  pass_through = [
    ["std::allocator", "memory"],
    ["std::char_traits", "string"],
  ]
  ```

  If a `[types]` section is present, `pass_through` must be present as a list,
  even if it is empty.

`[headers] system_paths`
  Include directories passed to the embedded Clang compiler as system header
  paths. These should normally be compiler, C++ standard library, libc, and
  package-manager include roots discovered by `config_gen.py`.

  Example:

  ```
  [headers]
  system_paths = [
    "/nix/store/...-gcc/include/c++/13.3.0",
    "/nix/store/...-glibc-dev/include",
  ]
  ```

  Treat these as the compiler/toolchain environment. They should not usually
  point at the target application's source tree.

`[headers] user_paths`
  Include directories passed to the embedded Clang compiler as user include
  paths. Use this for project or application headers needed by generated code
  and by container descriptors.

  Example:

  ```
  [headers]
  user_paths = [
    "/home/jon/Code/rocksdb",
    "/home/jon/Code/rocksdb/include",
  ]
  ```

  This is the place to add source trees for third-party binaries being
  introspected. If a descriptor says `header = "util/autovector.h"`, OIL needs
  a `user_paths` entry whose include tree contains `util/autovector.h`.


`[headers] preprocessor_defines`
  Preprocessor macros emitted into generated C++ before any `#include`
  directives. Entries use compiler `-D` style syntax: either `NAME` or
  `NAME=VALUE`. `NAME` is emitted as `#define NAME 1`.

  Example:

  ```
  [headers]
  preprocessor_defines = [
    "ROCKSDB_PLATFORM_POSIX=1",
    "OS_LINUX=1",
    "ROCKSDB_LIB_IO_POSIX=1",
  ]
  ```

  Use this for project build defines required by headers named in container
  descriptors. The values should match the binary being introspected; choosing
  different defines can make headers compile with a layout or API that does not
  match the debug information in the target binary.
